WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
A private model does not automatically make private use safe.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.115
Users, service accounts, applications, agents, administrators,

00:00:07.115 --> 00:00:09.322
and support tools all need appropriate permissions.

00:00:09.322 --> 00:00:13.320
A user may be allowed to ask general questions but not access sensitive document

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.120
collections,

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.960
call production tools, export logs, or trigger automated actions.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.250
Access control should cover more than the front door.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.160
Teams need rules for model access, retrieval collection access, tool permissions,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.577
administrative functions, service identities, and audit trails.

00:00:27.577 --> 00:00:29.450
Role-based access can be useful,

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:33.060
but AI systems often benefit from capability-style permissions as well:

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.120
this user can summarize approved documents,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.590
that service can call a specific retrieval index,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.980
and this workflow requires approval before an action is taken.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.310
Policy layers can decide which model is allowed, which data class may be used,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.070
which tools are enabled, which outputs require review,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.940
and when human approval is required.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.180
These decisions should be close enough to the workflow that they are testable and

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:52.920
reviewable.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.639
Sensitive actions should fail closed when policy context is missing or unclear.

00:00:56.639 --> 00:01:00.225
Agents and tool-using systems need especially clear boundaries because they can perform

00:01:00.225 --> 00:01:01.960
work beyond text generation.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.440
They may read, write, search, call APIs, open tickets, or trigger business processes.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.290
Least privilege, administrative separation, approval gates,

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.730
and structured audit records help keep those capabilities accountable.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.010
Sovereign AI depends on visible policy enforcement,

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.760
not informal trust that every user and tool will behave within expectations.
